.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt STAMP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm stamp
.Nd send STAMP test packets
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl T
.Op Fl C Ar source Ns Op : Ns Ar error
.Op Fl c Ar count
.Op Fl i Ar interval
.Op Fl l Ar length
.Op Fl m Cm stateless | stateful
.Op Fl p Ar port
.Op Fl q Ar seq
.Op Fl S Ar sport
.Op Fl s Ar ssid
.Op Fl t Ar ttl
.Op Fl W Ar wait
.Ar host
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a Session-Sender of the Simple Two-way Active Measurement
Protocol (STAMP, RFC 8762) with RFC 8972's Session-Sender Identifier,
on
.Xr libstamp 3 Ns 's
sender core, for tests and by hand.
It sends
.Ar count
test packets to the Session-Reflector at
.Ar host ,
such as
.Xr stampd 8 ,
one every
.Ar interval
whatever came back, and prints a line for each reply as it comes, or
.Ql none
for one that did not come within its wait.
It sends in the FIB it runs in
.Pq Xr setfib 1 .
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "-i interval"
.It Fl T
Ask for the reflector's Timestamp Information TLV (RFC 8972 section
4.3): the packet carries it after the base, and what
.Fl l
adds beyond it is an Extra Padding TLV (the packet 52 octets at least,
and 56 or more when longer).
.It Fl C Ar source Ns Op : Ns Ar error
This host's clock as it is, for one that
.Xr ntp_gettime 2
does not know is synchronised, as
.Xr stampd 8 Ns 's
.Fl C .
.It Fl c Ar count
Packets to send; default 1.
.It Fl i Ar interval
Seconds from one packet to the next; default 1.
.It Fl l Ar length
The packet's length; default 44, the base.
A longer one is padded with a pattern, which must come back; a shorter
one is no STAMP packet (a TWAMP Light sender's bare 14 octets) and
must not be answered.
.It Fl m Cm stateless | stateful
The reflector's mode, when known; else its first reply tells.
.It Fl p Ar port
The reflector's UDP port; default 862.
.It Fl q Ar seq
The first sequence number; default a random one, not 0, so that the
first reply tells a stateful reflector (whose new session counts from
0) from a stateless one.
Given, the mode may be told wrong unless
.Fl m
says it.
.It Fl S Ar sport
The UDP port to send from; default any.
.It Fl s Ar ssid
The Session-Sender Identifier; default 0.
.It Fl t Ar ttl
The packets' TTL or hop limit; default the system's.
.It Fl W Ar wait
Seconds to wait for a reply; default 2.
A reply later is lost.
.El
.Pp
A reply's line has these fields:
.Bl -tag -width "echo=ok"
.It Cm len
Its length.
.It Cm from
The address it came from.
.It Cm seq , ssid , sseq
The reflector's sequence number, the SSID, and the sender's sequence
number echoed.
.It Cm ttl , rttl
The TTL the packet reached the reflector with, and the reply's.
.It Cm echo
The sender's timestamp and error estimate came back.
.It Cm order
The reflector received it (T2) no later than it replied (T3).
.It Cm clock
Both within 5 seconds of this host's clock.
.It Cm serr
The reflector's error estimate has a multiplier.
.It Cm mbz
Its MBZ octets are zero.
.It Cm pad
It is as long as the packet, what followed the base come back.
.It Cm rtt , fwd , back
Milliseconds: the round trip less the reflector's time, (T4 \- T1) \-
(T3 \- T2), T1 and T4 on the monotonic clock; and the delay each way.
.It Cm owd
.Cm measured
when the delay each way is by the two clocks, the way out (T2 \- T1)
and back (T4 \- T3), T1 and T4 on the time of day: both error
estimates say synchronised, and the two errors together are no more
than a tenth of half the round trip, neither way negative;
.Cm estimated
when not, each way half the round trip.
.It Cm err
Milliseconds: the two error estimates together.
.It Cm ti
The reflector's Timestamp Information, with
.Fl T :
its clock's source and how it took the time the packet came, then the
same of the time it replied, as
.Ql ntp/sw,ntp/sw
(sources
.Cm ntp , ptp , ssu , gnss , local ;
methods
.Cm hw , sw , cp ) ;
.Ql -
without.
.El
.Pp
With a count above 1, a last line says the packets
.Cm sent ,
.Cm received
and
.Cm lost ;
from a stateful reflector, whose sequence number counts what reached
it, the loss each way:
.Cm forward
(never reached it),
.Cm backward
(reflected, the reply not back in time) and
.Cm unknown
(before the first reply, after the last, or across a reflector that
started again); from a stateless one
.Ql forward=- backward=- ,
the round trip's alone;
.Cm reflector ,
its mode;
.Cm rtt ,
the round trips' least, mean and most;
.Cm fwd
and
.Cm back ,
the delay each way, the mean; and
.Cm measured ,
how many replies' delay each way was by the two clocks.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 when a reply came, 2 when none did, and 1 on an error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ stamp -c 100 -i 0.1 192.0.2.1
$ setfib 1 stamp -s 7 -l 200 2001:db8::1
$ stamp -T -C ptp:0.000001 -c 10 192.0.2.1
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr setfib 1 ,
.Xr libstamp 3 ,
.Xr stampd 8
